{"reference":"2 Corinthians 10","book":"2 Corinthians","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Authority, Reputation, and Limits","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Paul defends an authority he says was given to build the Corinthians up rather than tear them down (v. 8). That claim supplies a standard for assessing his authority, but it does not place his threats, rhetoric, or effects beyond evaluation. Likewise, his refusal to compare himself with self-commending rivals and his appeal to an assigned sphere (vv. 12–18) address contested reputation and boundaries of work; they do not make communities the possession of a leader or justify self-promotion as proof of approval.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:1","2 Corinthians 10:12-18","2 Corinthians 10:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_010_f6c85e59e784b2f3817a6f3c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The charge in verse 10 contrasts forceful letters with a supposedly weak bodily presence and unimpressive speech. The chapter reports that accusation and answers it by insisting that Paul’s conduct in person will match his letters. It should not be used to diagnose Paul or to rank people by appearance, speech, affect, disability, or communication style.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:1-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_010_83a72a86eecaaedde3beb868"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Warfare as Argument","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The warfare language in verses 3–6 is framed by meekness and gentleness, by life “in the flesh,” and by a denial that the conflict is waged according to the flesh. The “strongholds” are immediately explained in terms of arguments, pretensions, and thoughts raised against the knowledge of God. This confines the imagery to an argumentative conflict rather than treating people, places, illnesses, disabilities, or other religions as enemy territory.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:1-4","2 Corinthians 10:4-5","2 Corinthians 10:5-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_010_55988ae262f136376b75755c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"“Taking every thought captive” is martial imagery used within an apostolic dispute. In this passage it cannot authorize mind control, censorship, anti-intellectualism, conspiracy thinking, or punishment for questions.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:3-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_010_df25645af46c78300577a5c4"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Reading Power Carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Paul’s severe language about captivity, obedience, and punishment should be read alongside his claim that the weapons are not fleshly and his appeal to meekness. The passage’s own explanation of strongholds as arguments keeps this rhetoric from supporting physical force, militarized ministry, harassment, surveillance, dehumanization, or treating opponents as targets.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:1-6","2 Corinthians 10:12-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_010_65616ffc2c5cd787e0ff1fd4"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Wider Conflict","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"In the following chapters, Paul continues to contest rival standards through accounts of hardship, weakness, and reluctant boasting. Because this remains a rhetorically interested defense within an unresolved conflict, it should not be read as a simple résumé or as a model for leader grandiosity, founder immunity, suffering competitions, or retaliatory discipline.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 11:1-33","2 Corinthians 12:1-13","2 Corinthians 12:1-21","2 Corinthians 13:1-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_010_92a8da8a3ba93bc1774efd61"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"The wider letter is preserved in multiple manuscript witnesses: P46 contains most of 2 Corinthians, P99 preserves portions, and major codices supply further witnesses with distinct textual profiles.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 11:1-33","2 Corinthians 12:1-21","2 Corinthians 13:1-14","2 Corinthians 1:1-24","2 Corinthians 2:1-17","2 Corinthians 3:1-18","2 Corinthians 4:1-18","2 Corinthians 5:1-21","2 Corinthians 6:1-18","2 Corinthians 7:1-16","2 Corinthians 8:1-24","2 Corinthians 9:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_010_1e046392e5f598c82157ea12"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}